---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/guides/deliverability';
const title = 'SMS deliverability and carrier filtering';
const desc = 'When Shopify texts fail, separate audience exclusions, queue delays and transport errors before changing the campaign. A sender accepting a request does not prove that a person received or read it.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">SMS operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-12">

<h2>Find the stage where the message stopped</h2>
<p>Start with one campaign and a defined time window. Export recipient eligibility, queue timestamps, provider message IDs, status callbacks, destination country and error codes where available. Redact phone numbers in shared incident notes and keep the secure originals available to authorized support. Compare against the last comparable send, not an unrelated broadcast with a different audience mix.</p>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Observed state</th><th>First investigation</th><th>Action to avoid</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Excluded before send</td><td>Consent, suppression, frequency and timezone rules</td><td>Disabling safeguards to raise the sent count</td></tr>
<tr><td>Queued without provider acceptance</td><td>Schedule, throughput, account limits and integration backlog</td><td>Creating another broadcast that duplicates the queue</td></tr>
<tr><td>Undelivered with an error</td><td>Exact provider code, sender, destination and content</td><td>Assuming every failure is carrier filtering</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reported delivered, few clicks</td><td>Link function, audience relevance and reporting delay</td><td>Claiming delivery proves reading or purchase intent</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Read codes in the supplier's own dictionary</h2>
<p><a class="link" href="https://www.twilio.com/docs/api/errors/30007">Twilio error 30007</a> means filtering by Twilio or a carrier. It does not identify a universal banned word or prove that changing one phrase will fix the problem. Other suppliers may use different codes. Preserve the original code and the provider's explanation in your incident record.</p>
<p>For a Twilio 30007 support case, its documentation asks for at least three undelivered message IDs with that error. A Shopify app may require you to escalate through its support team instead of contacting the underlying carrier or infrastructure provider yourself. Ask for the route and data they need; do not invent an upstream relationship from an app's branding.</p>
<h2>Review sender registration and message purpose</h2>
<p>Confirm the sending number, registration or verification status required for its route, brand identity and approved use case with the provider. Check that the campaign actually matches that purpose. Registration is an access requirement where applicable, not a guarantee against filtering. Do not rotate numbers or disguise a promotion to evade enforcement.</p>
<p><a class="link" href="https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/messaging-policy">Twilio's policy</a> covers consent and acceptable messaging across its services. Check your actual provider's current policy and destination restrictions as well. Legal sale of an item does not alone establish that a messaging route accepts its promotion. Escalate category questions before a launch rather than treating a blocked campaign as a copywriting puzzle.</p>
<h2>Inspect the complete message and destination</h2>
<p>Archive the exact body, encoding, media, tracking URL, redirects and final landing page. Check that links work on a phone, the brand is recognizable, the destination matches the promised offer, and no unexpected redirect or expired campaign page intervenes. A shortened or tracked link should be reviewed with the sender; do not claim a branded domain automatically bypasses carrier filters.</p>
<p>Compare failure patterns across sender, country, carrier where known, message version and time. A broad spike after a new URL is a lead to investigate, not causal proof. Keep unrelated variables steady in any proposed retest. One successful seed-device message cannot establish deliverability across a subscriber base.</p>
<h2>Retries need a reason and an expiry</h2>
<p>Classify retryable conditions using provider guidance. Invalid destinations, revoked permission and policy blocks should not receive blind retries. For transient failures, bound retries to the relevance window of the message and deduplicate by campaign, customer and intent. Recheck purchase, inventory and consent before a delayed reminder goes out.</p>
<p>If you pause a queue, record whether the platform will resume it automatically. Assign an owner to cancel stale sale or cart messages before reactivation. Do not resend an entire campaign merely because callbacks are delayed; first reconcile accepted requests and final statuses so already delivered texts are not duplicated.</p>
<h2>Use a diagnostic report with explicit denominators</h2>
<p>Track attempted provider submissions, final delivered statuses, undelivered statuses, pending/unknown statuses and pre-send exclusions separately. State the observation cutoff. Calculate a delivered share only against the stated submitted population; do not count a consent exclusion as a carrier failure. Review opt-outs and complaints separately from transport errors.</p>
<p>For a proposed controlled retest, record the original hypothesis, approved change, eligible audience, maximum exposure, observation window and rollback owner. Do not describe the retest as successful until its evidence exists. There is no universal delivery-rate promise or safe broadcast volume in this guide.</p>
<h2>Evaluate observability before switching apps</h2>
<p>Ask candidates for message-level exports, error explanations, callback latency, registration support, queue controls and incident escalation ownership. Compare those capabilities with the current failure before migrating; a new platform cannot repair missing permission records. See <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent collection</a> for that separate task.</p>
<p><a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">Sequenzy advertises SMS/MMS in beta</a> on paid plans with a US/Canada toll-free number. Confirm message-level diagnostics, registration process and beta support commitments directly. Its email features alone do not establish SMS transport performance, and this guide has not conducted a delivery comparison.</p>
<p>Close an incident with the affected population, error distribution, confirmed cause or remaining uncertainty, corrective action and evidence from any later retest. Use <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing</a> to reconcile failed-message charges against the contract; failed delivery does not by itself establish whether a supplier bills the attempt.</p>

    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
